{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>בְּנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים</b> — the sons of the princes and judges, this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 6:2), and so it is in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 26:5). If so, Scripture relates that the judges who were theirs to do justice — their sons commit violence openly, and no one stops them." "<b>כִּי טוֹבוֹת הֵנָּה</b> — like 'כִּי תִרְאֶה חֲמוֹר שֹׂנַאֲךָ' (Exodus 23:5), 'כִּי יִקָּרֵא קַן צִפּוֹר לְפָנֶיךָ' (Deuteronomy 22:6): when they are good-looking, they take them for themselves as wives by force. And Scripture relates the violence, and further says 'מִכֹּל אֲשֶׁר בָּחָרוּ,' to include married women to others. But Scripture did not mention the prohibition in them explicitly, and the punishment was decreed upon them only for the violence, because it is a matter graspable by reason — we do not need the Torah for it."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Reading לָהֶם as 'who were theirs' (i.e., the judges responsible for justice); the syntax is elliptical."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:25:12.683656853Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}